<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 1: </span>Thematic instantiations of the core LieCraft framework. While the underlying game mechanics remain identical across all variants, narrative framing and social context are adapted to reflect domain-specific ethical tensions and realistic decision-making scenarios.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="Sx3.SSx3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Thematic Modularity</h3>

<div id="Sx3.SSx3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Sx3.SSx3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">One of the primary challenges in studying deception in LLMs is ensuring that observed behavior generalizes beyond toy examples and contrived scripts. To address this, we introduce a thematic modularity into the LieCraft framework, in which the underlying mechanics of LieCraft are embedded in multiple high-stakes, every-day domain-specific scenarios. These themes are designed to simulate realistic ethical dilemmas, from infrastructure allocation to corporate malfeasance, to provide the setting in which we can observe models engaging in plausible, context-dependent deception.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx3.SSx3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Sx3.SSx3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The use of themes are motivated by three goals: 1) <span id="Sx3.SSx3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Mundane Realism</span>:
As themes ground the game play to
common subjects (e.g., hospitals, law enforcement, fairness, efficiency), emergent behaviors are readily interpretable and applicable to real deployment settings; 2) <span id="Sx3.SSx3.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Strategic Framing of Roles</span>: In each theme, players choose between role analogs of cooperators and defectors (e.g., cooperative vs. adversarial alignments).
Defectors act covertly with plausible deniability, while cooperators coordinate and signal trust—mirroring real-world scenarios of deception and strategic ambiguity; and 3) <span id="Sx3.SSx3.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Controlled Generalization</span>: Because each theme reuses the same game structure (number of rounds, player roles, voting mechanics), we can isolate how domain framing affects model behavior.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx3.SSx3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Sx3.SSx3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">With that in mind, we develop ten themes, each mapping the roles, assets, and objectives of the core game to a new domain.
Table <a href="#Sx3.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ Probability Model for Random Sabotage ‣ The LieCraft Framework ‣ LieCraft: A Multi-Agent Framework for Evaluating Deceptive Capabilities in Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> summarizes the key conflicts simulated by each theme.
In each case, we rewrite game rules and narrative framing to reflect domain-specific norms while preserving structural consistency.
This lets us measure how LLMs adapt their deceptive behavior to the affordances of each environment.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="Sx4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section">Experiments</h2>

<div id="Sx4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Sx4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Given the scale of LieCraft as a framework for measuring multi-agent interactions, it provides a rich testbed for measuring deception propensity, success, and detection accuracy. We report experiments and analyses that support our driving research questions.</p>
</div>
<section id="Sx4.SSx3.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Evaluated Models</h5>

<div id="Sx4.SSx3.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Sx4.SSx3.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In our experiments we evaluate 12 popular, state-of-the-art, LLMs comprising open-source, as well as proprietary models. For open models, we selected 8 in total: two from each of the 4 most commonly-used families: Qwen, Deepseek, Gemma, and Llama. We also evaluate 4 proprietary models: o4-mini, GPT-4o, Gemini-2.5-Flash, and Claude-3.7 Sonnet. For each open model family, we selected the largest models that were feasible to run, i.e. <math id="Sx4.SSx3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\leq 100" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mn>100</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\leq 100</annotation></semantics></math>B parameters. We believe the 32B+ parameter range represents a favorable cost-intelligence tradeoff, and thus is most commonly deployed in production environments. Hence, results on models of this scale in addition to proprietary models are most relevant to current systems<span id="footnote3" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">‡</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">‡</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">‡</span>
              
              
            We find that <math id="footnote3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\leq 20" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mn>20</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\leq 20</annotation></semantics></math>B parameter models struggle to play the game, such as directly shouting ”I AM A DEFECTOR!”</span></span></span>. Due to cost limitations we do not evaluate on the strongest models: Claude-4 Opus, OpenAI’s o3, or Gemini-2.5-Pro.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx4.SSx3.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Game State Prompting</h5>

<div id="Sx4.SSx3.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Sx4.SSx3.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">While humans can play LieCraft by looking at visual elements and drawing on past game experience, LLMs require prompt-engineering to be able to play the game with any measure of skill. We detail our prompting setup in the supplementary material, but at a high level, we find the following steps to be necessary to ensure smooth gameplay for all models: 1) include the rules of the game in the system prompt; 2) include a minimal representation of the game state within the main body of the prompt, where the game state includes all public information available to the player, as well as any past scratchpad entries and a minified history of the game; and 3) use structured (json) outputs where possible to constrain generation to valid text.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx4.SSx3.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Sx4.SSx3.SSS0.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Every action is sent to the LLM-player as a structured json object with 2 components: an open-ended string called ”note2self”, followed by a constrained action (playing a card, role selection, etc.). A full example of the prompt, from an in progress game, is shown in the supplementary material.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx4.SSx3.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Common Experimental Parameters</h5>

<div id="Sx4.SSx3.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Sx4.SSx3.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As seen in Figure <a href="#Sx1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ Introduction ‣ LieCraft: A Multi-Agent Framework for Evaluating Deceptive Capabilities in Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, LieCraft has many parameters that can be varied to induce different player dynamics. Unless otherwise indicated, we select a common set of parameters and hold them constant across all experiments.
<span id="Sx4.SSx3.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Game Structure</span>: all games consist of 3 missions of 5 events each. For each game we sample rewards as follows: <math id="Sx4.SSx3.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{start}\sim\{1,2,3,4\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{start}\sim\{1,2,3,4\}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Sx4.SSx3.SSS0.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S\sim\{3,4,5\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S\sim\{3,4,5\}</annotation></semantics></math> are uniformly sampled from constraint-satisfying combinations, <math id="Sx4.SSx3.SSS0.Px3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> is sampled uniformly from <math id="Sx4.SSx3.SSS0.Px3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[R_{start}+S-1,R_{start}+S+1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[R_{start}+S-1,R_{start}+S+1]</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="Sx4.SSx3.SSS0.Px3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{n}\sim\{0,1,2\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{n}\sim\{0,1,2\}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="Sx4.SSx3.SSS0.Px3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{c}=B_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{c}=B_{d}</annotation></semantics></math>.
This sampling procedure ensures players are properly incentivized to choose both roles. Finally, all rewards are multiplied by the mission <math id="Sx4.SSx3.SSS0.Px3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> to emphasize later missions where the models have experience playing.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx4.SSx3.SSS0.Px3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Sx4.SSx3.SSS0.Px3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For each theme, mission cards are drawn from a fixed deck containing 10 possible scenarios, and event cards are drawn from a fixed deck of size 20. We allow the models extra discussion phases (2 per vote phase, +2 per nomination phase) purely for the purposes of data collection.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx4.SSx3.SSS0.Px3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Sx4.SSx3.SSS0.Px3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Sx4.SSx3.SSS0.Px3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">LLM parameters</span>: all generations are acquired using a vLLM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kwon et al. 2023</a>)</cite> server for open models, and the respective API for proprietary models. Temperature is set to 1.0 for all models for all generations except o4-mini, which does not use the temperature setting. Overall, we follow API based default configurations to reflect standard usage scenarios. Additionally, we enforce a 32K max context length for all open models, and further control the context length by replacing the game history with a generated summary after each mission <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Light et al. 2023</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx4.SSx3.SSS0.Px3.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Sx4.SSx3.SSS0.Px3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Finally, we run over 1000 multiplayer games in total across 12 models and 10 themes. Games are formed by uniformly sampling models without replacement in blocks of 5. We run games for all scenarios until we have at least 30 games played for each model across every theme.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx4.SSx3.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Accusation Skill Metric</h5>

<div id="Sx4.SSx3.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Sx4.SSx3.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate accusation accuracy using a difficulty-adjusted scoring system.
Given <math id="Sx4.SSx3.SSS0.Px4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=4</annotation></semantics></math> other players with <math id="Sx4.SSx3.SSS0.Px4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> defectors, a cooperator’s accusation score is:
<math id="Sx4.SSx3.SSS0.Px4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{\text{correct}}=\frac{n}{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mtext>correct</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mi>n</mi><mi>d</mi></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{\text{correct}}=\frac{n}{d}</annotation></semantics></math> for correctly identifying a defector (rewarding harder identifications where <math id="Sx4.SSx3.SSS0.Px4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> is small);
<math id="Sx4.SSx3.SSS0.Px4.p1.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="S_{\text{false}}=-1\cdot(1+\frac{d}{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mtext>false</mtext></msub><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></mfrac><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{\text{false}}=-1\cdot(1+\frac{d}{n})</annotation></semantics></math> for falsely accusing a cooperator when <math id="Sx4.SSx3.SSS0.Px4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="Sx4.SSx3.SSS0.Px4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d=0</annotation></semantics></math> otherwise;
and <math id="Sx4.SSx3.SSS0.Px4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{\text{unknown}}=1-\frac{2d}{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mtext>unknown</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mi>n</mi></mfrac></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{\text{unknown}}=1-\frac{2d}{n}</annotation></semantics></math> for declining to accuse (optimal when <math id="Sx4.SSx3.SSS0.Px4.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d=0</annotation></semantics></math>, suboptimal as <math id="Sx4.SSx3.SSS0.Px4.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> increases).
This formulation ensures scores reflect performance relative to random guessing, with <math id="Sx4.SSx3.SSS0.Px4.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{\text{correct}}\in[1,4]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mtext>correct</mtext></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{\text{correct}}\in[1,4]</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="Sx4.SSx3.SSS0.Px4.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{\text{false}}\in[-1.75,-0.25]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mtext>false</mtext></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1.75</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>0.25</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{\text{false}}\in[-1.75,-0.25]</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="Sx4.SSx3.SSS0.Px4.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{\text{unknown}}\in[-1,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mtext>unknown</mtext></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{\text{unknown}}\in[-1,1]</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="Sx4.F2" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2603.06874v1/global_ts.svg" id="Sx4.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:698/575;" width="698" height="575"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2: </span>TrueSkill ranking of all models evaluated in LieCraft. The order indicates overall rank, while <math id="Sx4.F2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Sx4.F2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> indicate skill level and uncertainty respectively</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="Sx4.F3" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2603.06874v1/role_select_heatmap.png" id="Sx4.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:698/605;" width="698" height="605" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 3: </span><span id="Sx4.F3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">RQ1:</span> Role selection rates across models and themes. We find a diverse behavior across models. We set the midpoint of the colormap to <math id="Sx4.F3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.75" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.75</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.75</annotation></semantics></math> to reflect the relative risk of models choosing unethical alignments.</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="Sx4.F4" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2603.06874v1/win_rate_vs_accusations.svg" id="Sx4.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:698/414;" width="698" height="414"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 4: </span><span id="Sx4.F4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">RQ2, RQ3:</span> defector sabotage rates (outperforming cooperators) versus accusation score.As models improve at identifying liars, their ability to deceive also increases.</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="Sx4.F5" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2603.06874v1/successful_unsafe_lies_proportion_gpt.svg" id="Sx4.F5.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:698/330;" width="698" height="330"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 5: </span>Proportion of types of (successful) deceptive speech displayed by each model in the multiplayer setting.</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="Sx4.F6" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2603.06874v1/unsafe_proportion_base_vs_unsafe.svg" id="Sx4.F6.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:698/350;" width="698" height="350"></object></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2603.06874v1/combined_family_rates.svg" id="Sx4.F6.g2" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:698/209;" width="698" height="209"></object></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 6: </span>Differences in LieCraft gameplay between base models and abliterated (unsafe) variants. <span id="Sx4.F6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Top:</span> Change in preferred deceptive speech types. <span id="Sx4.F6.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Bottom:</span> Change in win rate and defector selection rates.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="Sx4.SSx1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">TrueSkill Ranking</h3>

<div id="Sx4.SSx1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Sx4.SSx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We use the TrueSkill scoring system <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Herbrich, Minka, and Graepel 2006</a>)</cite> to assess model skill in LieCraft. Quantifying skill allows us to rank models in terms how effective they at executing their agendas in a multi-agent configuration, in service of their particular ethical alignment. Well-known skill estimation frameworks like Elo or Bradley-Terry models are more suited for head-to-head matches than free-for-all multiplayer games. TrueSkill is a Bayesian rating system designed to handle arbitrary match formats, including multiplayer free-for-all. TrueSkill assumes a common prior over player skill, <math id="Sx4.SSx1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{0}\sim\mathcal{N}(25,\frac{25}{3})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>25</mn><mo>,</mo><mfrac><mn>25</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{0}\sim\mathcal{N}(25,\frac{25}{3})</annotation></semantics></math>, and updates a Gaussian posterior after each match. This yields a per-player point estimate <math id="Sx4.SSx1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math> along with an uncertainty measure <math id="Sx4.SSx1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math>. The authors of TrueSkill offer guidelines for how many matches per-player per-game are required to estimate true skill (<math id="Sx4.SSx1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5</annotation></semantics></math>), we run more than double this number of games, giving us more than 100 games per theme. Each game gives us a final ranking that we use to estimate the corresponding skills of each player, we show the full skill breakdown in Figure <a href="#Sx4.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ Accusation Skill Metric ‣ Experiments ‣ LieCraft: A Multi-Agent Framework for Evaluating Deceptive Capabilities in Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>. Surprisingly, while Claude-3.7 is rank 1, the proprietary models are not categorically the most skilled players. Indeed, Gemini-2.5-flash and o4-mini are in the bottom half of the rankings. More details on our skill estimation process and TrueSkill hyperparameter selection, as well as theme-specific skill rankings can be found in the supplementary material.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx4.SSx2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Answering LieCraft Research Questions</h3>

<div id="Sx4.SSx2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Sx4.SSx2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our experiments address the three research questions:</p>
<ul id="Sx4.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="Sx4.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="Sx4.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Sx4.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Sx4.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">RQ1 Deception Propensity:</span> What is the frequency of defector role selection when given the choice?</p>
</div></li>
<li id="Sx4.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="Sx4.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Sx4.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Sx4.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">RQ2 Deception Success:</span> What is the rate of avoiding detection when successfully playing the defector role?</p>
</div></li>
<li id="Sx4.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="Sx4.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx4.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Sx4.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">RQ3 Detection Ability:</span> How well can models correctly identify defectors?</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="Sx4.SSx2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Sx4.SSx2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#Sx4.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ Accusation Skill Metric ‣ Experiments ‣ LieCraft: A Multi-Agent Framework for Evaluating Deceptive Capabilities in Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> shows the results of our experiment answering <span id="Sx4.SSx2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">RQ1</span>. We find a wide range of role selection behaviors across our tested models, with Claude rarely choosing defector (except on the explicitly game-like default theme) and Gemini nearly always picking the defector role.
Figure <a href="#Sx4.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ Accusation Skill Metric ‣ Experiments ‣ LieCraft: A Multi-Agent Framework for Evaluating Deceptive Capabilities in Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> shows our results for <span id="Sx4.SSx2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">RQ2</span> and <span id="Sx4.SSx2.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">RQ3</span>, we find that in general, as accusation ability increases, so too does skill at playing defector. We find that Claude is the best defector when it chooses to be one, and that Gemini is the most accurate defector predictor.
These results, when paired with the TrueSkill performance, indicate that as models improve in skill, they become better not only at gameplay but also at lying and detecting lies.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx4.SSx3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Taxonomy of Deception</h3>

<div id="Sx4.SSx3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Sx4.SSx3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our results (<span id="Sx4.SSx3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">RQ1–3</span>) show all models willingly assume unethical roles in multi-agent play and exhibit varying skill at enacting and detecting deception. A natural question is <span id="Sx4.SSx3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">how</span> this deception occurs—what strategies each model uses to sabotage others. Deception most often arises in the pre-nomination discussion phase, when event cards are revealed and defectors must evade suspicion before accusations. Following <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Stepputtis et al. (2023)</a></cite>, we categorize deceptive text into three classes: <span id="Sx4.SSx3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">commission</span>, <span id="Sx4.SSx3.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">omission</span>, and <span id="Sx4.SSx3.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">influence</span>, adding a fourth: <span id="Sx4.SSx3.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">blame-shifting</span>, where defectors respond to accusations by accusing the accuser or another player.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx4.SSx3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Sx4.SSx3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We use LLM-as-Judge <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gu et al. 2024</a>)</cite> to measure how often each model deploys these strategies. We prompt GPT-4o with few-shot examples and game state to classify pre-nomination discussion into one deception type or a null label. Blind human evaluation on 100 randomly sampled deceptive cases shows high agreement (Cohen’s <math id="Sx4.SSx3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa&gt;0.8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0.8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa&gt;0.8</annotation></semantics></math>). Figure <a href="#Sx4.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ Accusation Skill Metric ‣ Experiments ‣ LieCraft: A Multi-Agent Framework for Evaluating Deceptive Capabilities in Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> summarizes the taxonomy: models exhibit distinct competencies across strategies, and all strongly favor <span id="Sx4.SSx3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">influence</span> over outright falsehoods. Unsuccessful deceptive speech frequencies are in the supplementary material.</p>
</div>
<figure id="Sx4.F7" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2603.06874v1/score_sensitivity_plot.svg" id="Sx4.F7.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:698/614;" width="698" height="614"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 7: </span>Model sensitivity to varying reward scaling factors, averaged across all themes.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="Sx4.SSx4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Misaligned Model Behavior</h3>

<div id="Sx4.SSx4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Sx4.SSx4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We also analyze models that have had their safety guardrails removed, investigating if they differ from the base models quantitatively with regard to deception. Specifically, we use <span id="Sx4.SSx4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">abliterated</span> models, or models that have been augmented to reduce their propensity to refuse instructions. As <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Arditi et al. (2024)</a></cite> has shown refusal is mediated by a one-dimensional subspace across many models, we follow their work to abliterate different LLMs by orthogonalizing their weights with respect to the feature direction that points toward refusal, where this direction is computed via steering with contrastive prompts: taking the difference of means of latents between refused and non-refused prompts.
We use abliterated analogs from each of our open model families: Qwen, Llama, Deepseek, and Gemma. To evaluate each model, we run multiplayer games on all themes in a 1-4 composition of abliterated-to-base models. Specifically, we look for changes along the following axes. 1) Changes to role-selection rate; 2) changes in types of deceptive speech; and 3) win-rate of abliterated models. In Figure <a href="#Sx4.F6" title="Figure 6 ‣ Accusation Skill Metric ‣ Experiments ‣ LieCraft: A Multi-Agent Framework for Evaluating Deceptive Capabilities in Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> we see that defection rates increase substantially for Gemma and Deepseek models, while changing relatively less for Qwen and Llama models. We also see that abliterated models undergo a shift in their propensity to use different deception strategies e.g., direct strategies like commission and omission become more likely. Finally, we note that while abliterated models demonstrate less ethical alignment overall, they seem significantly worse at executing their goals, which may be a boon for production systems.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx4.SSx5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Response to Reward Scaling</h3>

<div id="Sx4.SSx5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Sx4.SSx5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Finally, we test the open models’ sensitivity to scaling reward values and observe resulting changes in role selection across all themes. We run two experiments: scaling all rewards <math id="Sx4.SSx5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐑</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{R}</annotation></semantics></math> or just the defector-accused reward <math id="Sx4.SSx5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> by factor <math id="Sx4.SSx5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda=10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda=10</annotation></semantics></math>. Figure <a href="#Sx4.F7" title="Figure 7 ‣ Taxonomy of Deception ‣ Experiments ‣ LieCraft: A Multi-Agent Framework for Evaluating Deceptive Capabilities in Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a> shows the change in models selecting defector when scaling by <math id="Sx4.SSx5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math>, averaged across themes.
Most models show limited sensitivity to scaling all rewards but can be sensitive to specific changes, suggesting models properly engage with our reward structure design.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="Sx5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section">Conclusion</h2>

<div id="Sx5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Sx5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Through extensive evaluation of 12 popular, state-of-the-art LLMs across 10 thematic variations of our framework LieCraft, we reveal new patterns in deceptive behavior. Claude 3.7 Sonnet emerges as simultaneously the most honest and capable model, achieving the highest TrueSkill rating (33.7), selecting deceptive roles least frequently (<math id="Sx5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sim" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∼</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sim</annotation></semantics></math>5%), but is also the most capable deceiver when choosing so (<math id="Sx5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="48" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>48</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">48</annotation></semantics></math>% defector win rate). In contrast, models like Gemma3-27b (TrueSkill: 21.7) struggle across all dimensions.
Our findings carry sobering implications: all tested models willingly adopt unethical roles and use sophisticated deception strategies. The connection between accusation skill and deception success indicates these capabilities develop in tandem, not opposition. Models that excel at detecting deception also tend to produce it, the opposite of the safety profile one would hope for.
We observed systematic preferences in role selection among proprietary models, Gemini deceives less effectively than Claude yet chooses deceptive roles more often, which raises concerns for autonomous and multi-agent deployments.
LieCraft provides a critical tool for ongoing evaluation, but our results demonstrate the need for fundamental advances in creating trustworthy AI systems.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section">Acknowledgments</h2>

<div id="Sx6.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Sx6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We would like to thank Stefan Lee for his game design insights in early versions of the framework. We would also like to thank Gadi Singer for his support in building benchmarks for evaluating deception in LLMs.</p>
</div>
</section>
